[claude-9b] Released in setup tool 2.23.0 (2026-10-28), tested by claude-9b-test in s157 (frame captures in forest,
nightd, saint_lo, crssrds and mp_merv 4 players, and a full-size pop-in check); the game turns it on in
recomp/src/app_defaults.h. The text below and the "UNTESTED" marks in the code comments are from the draft.
[claude-9b-think] 2026-10-27 draft. SDK patch 0145, v2 (replaces v1, ba808aac): Vulkan
async pipelines without a placeholder made on the GPU thread, and without the wait at each submission's end, with
counters. Owner 19:31: "optimizting in-game performance and reducing/eliminating hitching is priority #1".

v2: v1 alone would have moved the stall, not removed it. VulkanPipelineCache::EndSubmission (pipeline_cache.cpp
777-803), at the end of every submission (about 5 a frame in the owner's perf lines), makes every pipeline still
queued on the GPU thread itself (CreateQueuedPipelinesOnProcessorThread, "to reduce warm-up latency") and then
waits for the pipeline threads to finish theirs. With no placeholder, the real pipeline (the vertex shader's
compile, 20-44 ms) would have been made or waited for there, in the same frame. Today the placeholder pays the
vertex shader's compile in ConfigurePipeline and the real pipeline (pixel shader and link, a few ms: the log's
10:07:18 frame is 43.8 ms with 37.2 ms of "pipelines") is waited for at the submission's end. v2 adds
vulkan_async_wait_at_submission (default true: as before; off: the queued pipelines stay with the pipeline
threads) and times that wait. Run B needs both switches.

Why (the owner's play today, cod3.log 09:38-10:10, RTX 3080, Linux, Vulkan, async_shader_compilation true):
of the 19 slow-frame lines in play (not counting level starts and two resolution-scale switches in F2), 14 had
the GPU thread making pipelines, 12 of them mostly that ("pipelines 17.4-67.2 ms" in frames of 25-72 ms).
With async creation, ConfigurePipeline first makes a placeholder pipeline on the GPU
thread (the vertex shader with a stand-in pixel shader: a whole pipeline compile, 20-44 ms each in the log's
timestamps, e.g. VS DB49AC9A5CE43406 10:07:18.094 -> 18.130, then the 43.8 ms frame with "pipelines 37.2 ms"),
then hands the real one to a pipeline thread. But IssueDraw never draws with a placeholder: it leaves out
every draw whose pipeline is a placeholder (command_processor.cpp 4248-4251) and, with
vulkan_async_skip_incomplete_frames (default on), doesn't show that frame. The placeholder's compile buys
nothing but the wait. The D3D12 backend already makes new pipelines on its threads only
(d3d12/pipeline_cache.cpp 1086-1104).

What it does:
  - vulkan_async_pipeline_placeholders (default true: as before). Off: a new pipeline gets no placeholder; it
    is marked as being made (is_placeholder with no pipeline yet), handed to the pipeline threads, and its
    draws are left out until it is in, as with a placeholder. A pipeline whose creation fails stays "being
    made" (its draws left out; the error is logged by EnsurePipelineCreated), instead of being retried on the
    GPU thread for every draw.
  - vulkan_async_depth_only_pipelines (default false): with placeholders off, pipelines with no pixel shader
    (depth only, shadows; they never had a placeholder, so they were always made on the GPU thread) go to the
    threads too.
  - vulkan_async_wait_at_submission (default true: as before). Off: a submission's end only wakes the pipeline
    threads; it neither makes the queued pipelines on the GPU thread nor waits for them. Safe because no
    command buffer holds a pipeline that isn't made yet (a draw without its pipeline is left out, and a
    placeholder is never bound, so destroying it later at any submission is safe). OnPrimaryBufferEnd
    submits early only while no pipeline is being made (CanEndSubmissionImmediately): unchanged, so a frame
    with pipelines in flight is submitted at its swap and at the points that must submit.
  - Counters, always on (a clock read per pipeline made, per submission end and per swap), and a line every
    vulkan_pipeline_stats_seconds (default 10; 0 = off) when any pipeline was made or any draw left out:
      "Vulkan pipelines over 10.0 s (N frames): N made on the GPU thread while the frame waited (N of them
       placeholders, N at submission ends; X ms, the longest X ms); submission ends X ms making and
       waiting for pipelines, the longest X ms; N handed to the pipeline threads (N without a placeholder),
       N made there (X ms); N draws left out while their pipeline was made, in N frames; N frames not
       shown, the longest hold X ms"
    "the longest hold" is how long the picture stayed the same (vulkan_async_skip_incomplete_frames): from
    the first frame not shown to the next one shown.
  - Reads is_placeholder before the pipeline (ConfigurePipeline, GetPipelineAndLayoutByHandle): a pipeline
    thread stores the pipeline and its layout, then clears the flag, so a cleared flag means both are in.
Not changed: shader translation (still on the GPU thread, 0.7-3.8 ms a frame in today's slow frames), the
pipeline storage files, D3D12.

Test (s157, see FINDINGS-HITCHES): the same scenes with
  A: as today;
  A2: --vulkan_async_wait_at_submission=false (placeholders still made: their compile stays, the wait goes);
  B: --vulkan_async_pipeline_placeholders=false --vulkan_async_wait_at_submission=false;
  C: B plus --vulkan_async_skip_incomplete_frames=false (no held pictures; a missing object for a few frames);
  D: C plus --vulkan_async_depth_only_pipelines=true.
Before each run, delete userdata/cache/shaders (the first visit is what's measured) and keep the graphics
driver's own shader cache out of it, or shaders it compiled before come back much faster than a first
compile (NVIDIA on Linux: __GL_SHADER_DISK_CACHE_PATH set to a new empty folder for the run, or
__GL_SHADER_DISK_CACHE=0);
cod3_log_detail verbose.

